WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:04.790
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:06.110
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:07.390
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:08.610
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:12.450
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:19.430
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:20.590
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:24.570
Welcome.

9
00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:28.870
Welcome back to The Return.

10
00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:31.690
We're returning with The Return.

11
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:32.610
Do you see what we did there?

12
00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:33.490
See that?

13
00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:34.730
I like that.

14
00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:36.790
We're geniuses.

15
00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:39.230
No one can top us.

16
00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:42.090
Guys, this is Season 3, Episode 13.

17
00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:49.690
This was written by Gene Stupnitzki, Lee Eisenberg, and Michael Schur, and directed by Greg Daniels.

18
00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.070
Jenna, let's have a summary.

19
00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.010
All right, here we go.

20
00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:55.270
Oscar returns to work.

21
00:00:55.530 --> 00:01:03.250
He's returning from his vacation to an elaborate Mexican-themed party that he didn't ask for and doesn't really want.

22
00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:05.790
That sounds about right.

23
00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:11.450
With Dwight gone, Andy fights hard to win Michael's favor but crosses the line.

24
00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:17.090
Jim and Pam team up to prank Andy and ultimately bait him into an angry meltdown.

25
00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:27.190
When Michael learns from Angela that Dwight's supposed disloyalty was actually just a misunderstanding, he goes to Staples to win Dwight back.

26
00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:30.030
And Dwight returns.

27
00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:31.570
It's a double return.

28
00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:32.990
And there it is.

29
00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:34.170
It's a double return.

30
00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:45.210
Jenna, I feel like if we were to title this episode from Angela Martin's point of view, I would like to title it Snow and Sadness.

31
00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:52.610
There are so many snowflakes and tears falling in this episode.

32
00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:55.150
There's some serious moping and snow.

33
00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:56.710
Yeah.

34
00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:58.670
It's so true.

35
00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:06.050
Well, Angela, that leads me very well to fast fact number one, which I called It's Snowing in Scranton.

36
00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.430
It was such a fun backdrop to the whole episode.

37
00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:10.890
I know.

38
00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:13.910
We also got a lot of fan questions about it.

39
00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:20.110
Elisa Schroeder, Mariana Gonzalez, Holly Gannett, and Paige Carter all said,

40
00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:22.810
In this episode, it is constantly snowing.

41
00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:23.790
How did you do it?

42
00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:25.370
Was there a video there?

43
00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:26.830
Did they use a green screen?

44
00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:30.910
Well, I mean, I think we all know who I asked about this.

45
00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:32.590
Please tell me you hit up Kentopedia.

46
00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:34.350
Oh, lady, you know I did.

47
00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:36.750
And you know he delivered because he's amazing.

48
00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:40.530
And Kent Zabornak, thank you so much for always being there for us.

49
00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:46.170
So first of all, on one of the first pages in the script, there is this note.

50
00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:47.070
And it says,

51
00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:49.950
Through the window, we see that it is snowing.

52
00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:51.390
Michael doesn't notice.

53
00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:54.810
It should be snowing throughout the entire episode.

54
00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:57.150
So this was noted in the script.

55
00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:58.690
The writers came up with this idea.

56
00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:01.570
It's going to pay off when Michael's car fills with snow.

57
00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:09.210
But we also just used to like to represent the season that the show would be in when it aired.

58
00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:13.690
So even though we shot this in October, it was going to air in like January or February.

59
00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:15.170
It would be winter in Scranton.

60
00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:16.610
I love it.

61
00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:22.230
I love that throughout the episode, you see people like having talking heads and it's just coming down.

62
00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:23.790
I just love that little detail.

63
00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:29.090
Well, here is what we did to make it snow in those talking heads, Angela.

64
00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:34.150
We used the same company that we used in the Christmas episode, the first Christmas episode.

65
00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:34.730
Remember them?

66
00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:35.490
Snow business?

67
00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:36.290
Mm-hmm.

68
00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:39.570
And they used this very old-fashioned technique.

69
00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:42.670
He said they've been using it for decades and they still use it today.

70
00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:48.250
They take like a large roller and they put it behind the window.

71
00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:53.410
And it has like, what's that thing that you put your pasta in to drain it?

72
00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:55.950
Or like a sifter when you do powdered sugar?

73
00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:57.310
Yes.

74
00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:04.370
So it's a giant rolling sifter that they fill with snow and then they just crank it.

75
00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:09.050
There's like a dude back there cranking fake snow like through a giant sifter?

76
00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:09.450
Yeah.

77
00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:13.870
There can be a dude or a lady or it can be on a little motor.

78
00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:19.070
It just spins endlessly and snow falls out of it until you fill it again.

79
00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:21.210
And he even sent me a picture of it.

80
00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:23.670
So I will put it up on our Office Ladies Pod Instagram.

81
00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:25.890
Oh, I want to see that picture.

82
00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:26.250
Yeah.

83
00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:31.370
And then this same company snow business would come and put all the snow and ice on the ground

84
00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:35.490
and they had this big blower machine where they would like blow it all over the bushes.

85
00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:38.750
I remember the blower machine, but I don't think I ever went

86
00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:42.610
back behind where the windows were where we did talking heads.

87
00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:44.250
And I never got to see the sifter.

88
00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:48.130
Well, there's a little gap, you know, there's like the wall and then there's a gap and then

89
00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:51.990
there's that Scranton backdrop that we would also change seasonally.

90
00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:57.150
So we had a snowy backdrop and then we had a little sifter fella.

91
00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:58.070
Our sifter person.

92
00:04:58.230 --> 00:04:59.310
Yeah, exactly.

93
00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:01.510
Well, I love that fast fact.

94
00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:06.810
Well, fast fact number two, Angela, remember last week we talked about how this episode

95
00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:13.190
and traveling salesman were shot as one big episode and our director of this episode and

96
00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:17.810
our showrunner, Greg Daniels, sent in a bunch of audio clips where he mentioned some of the

97
00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:19.450
inspiration for these two episodes.

98
00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:24.470
Well, he did it again and he sent in an audio clip about some of the things that they were

99
00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:27.170
juggling for this portion of the storyline.

100
00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:28.770
So, Sam, will you play that clip?

101
00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:32.090
These were like extremely complex storylines.

102
00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:36.290
There was a lot of good stuff between Pam and Angela, between Dwight and Angela, between

103
00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:37.230
Michael and Dwight.

104
00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:44.430
And these stories brought in lots of cards that we had on the boards.

105
00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:49.090
One of the things that we would do in pre-production is we would generate all these funny ideas

106
00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:58.890
for different storylines or fragments or B stories and they would become note cards that

107
00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:02.670
would be push-pinned into the cork boards.

108
00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:04.990
So, there's a lot of cards.

109
00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:08.930
I recognize cards in the stories that had been on the boards.

110
00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:15.950
Like, I think the Mexican-themed party, for instance, was a card for Oscar.

111
00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:25.710
And certainly, the Pam and Angela relationship or Andy being more sinister and Michael having

112
00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:32.370
to wise up and realize who his most loyal servant was.

113
00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:40.810
These are all, you know, fragmentary ideas that we combined into a great episode, I think.

114
00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:43.310
A lot of moving parts.

115
00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:48.390
Yeah, I love picturing the writer's room in these cards and them coming back around.

116
00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:51.370
And, Greg, I love when you send in audio clips.

117
00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:51.990
Thank you.

118
00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:52.570
I agree.

119
00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:54.690
Fast fact number three.

120
00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:56.810
Rid-a-dip-da-doo!

121
00:06:57.890 --> 00:06:59.010
Ed Helms!

122
00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:01.250
You heard her, everybody!

123
00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:03.750
We talked to Ed Helms about this episode!

124
00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:06.050
This is such a big episode for Andy.

125
00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:08.490
He is driving most of the storyline.

126
00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:09.850
He is the instigator.

127
00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:13.190
We absolutely had to talk to Ed, so we did, and it was great.

128
00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:14.050
It was so fun.

129
00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:15.190
Sam, play the interview.

130
00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:26.180
Helms!

131
00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:26.600
Hello!

132
00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:27.860
How are you?

133
00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:29.760
Hi, office ladies!

134
00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:32.060
Ed, it's so good to see you!

135
00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:34.980
You got an office guy right here in the mix.

136
00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:35.320
We do.

137
00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:36.620
We got an office fella.

138
00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:37.280
Yeah.

139
00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:39.020
We got one of the best office fellas.

140
00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:39.480
Aw.

141
00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:39.940
Aw.

142
00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:43.020
Well, thanks so much for being here, Ed.

143
00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:48.140
People just love Andy Bernard, and we're so excited to talk to you about this episode.

144
00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:55.460
Yeah, I rewatched a little bit just so I had it in my head for this chat, because I never

145
00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:58.900
watch it, unless I happen to see it on in the background somewhere.

146
00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:01.140
But I love it when I catch it.

147
00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:02.080
Yeah.

148
00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:08.620
Have you ever had that experience, though, where you're flying on an airplane, and you

149
00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:13.640
see it on all the screens, that people are watching it on all their personal devices and

150
00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:16.180
on the airplane, and then you're just sort of sitting there.

151
00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:17.980
Please don't notice me.

152
00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:19.000
Please don't notice me.

153
00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:21.340
Yeah, I'm always like, do I go to the bathroom?

154
00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:22.900
Do I not go to the bathroom?

155
00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:28.240
I was trying to time it out to go to the bathroom one time, and there was someone in there, so

156
00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:32.240
I was standing outside, and I was on the little screen above my head.

157
00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:32.780
Oh, my God.

158
00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:34.520
And I just started to see the faces.

159
00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:35.040
Look at me.

160
00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:35.460
Look up.

161
00:08:35.540 --> 00:08:35.880
Look at me.

162
00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:36.260
Look up.

163
00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:37.040
And I was like, oh, God.

164
00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:39.000
Oh, that's amazing.

165
00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:43.280
Angela, I would expect you to just sit down on someone's armrest and be like, hey, you

166
00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:43.780
having fun?

167
00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:44.820
You enjoying the episode?

168
00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:45.980
Well, you got any questions?

169
00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:47.960
I'm happy to talk about it.

170
00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:52.720
I've exchanged more email addresses with flight attendants and made good friends on

171
00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:52.980
planes.

172
00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:56.620
Literally, I sat next to a woman one time, and we're still friends to this day.

173
00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:58.920
We're total strangers flying to Canada.

174
00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:01.400
So, Ed, you watched part of The Return?

175
00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:02.500
Yeah.

176
00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:03.040
Yeah.

177
00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:04.460
I re-watched The Return.

178
00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:10.200
You know, I watched just a couple of episodes from that first chunk of season three, because

179
00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:13.360
that was my first time on the show.

180
00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:13.900
Yeah.

181
00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:18.540
And it feels so long ago.

182
00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:24.160
I can't – like, it's just not fresh in my mind, so many of those experiences.

183
00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:30.560
And so, re-watching it, I was hoping I could kind of trigger a lot of memories of kind of

184
00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:33.740
the experience and the emotional memories and so forth.

185
00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:36.140
And it was a joy.

186
00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:37.740
It was really, really fun.

187
00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:41.020
When I watch it, I get a sense memory of my desk.

188
00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:47.760
I know that they've torn it down now, and they do a lot of game shows where our set used

189
00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:48.160
to be.

190
00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:51.520
And I was even invited to go on one of them.

191
00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:56.940
And I turned it down because I didn't think that I could emotionally handle walking into

192
00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:00.540
the soundstage and not seeing Pam's desk.

193
00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:06.280
Like, wow, we – at Chandler Valley Center Studios, they do game shows now?

194
00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:06.820
Yes.

195
00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:07.100
Yeah.

196
00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:09.240
I did, to tell the truth.

197
00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:11.920
And when I got the address, I said, yes, I would do it.

198
00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:13.560
And, you know, I don't know where things tape.

199
00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:14.800
So, I was like, yeah, sure, I'll do it.

200
00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:18.800
And then I got the address, and it was Chandler Valley Studios.

201
00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:19.920
And I was like, what?

202
00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:23.300
I showed up at our studio, and I started sobbing.

203
00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:24.160
Oh.

204
00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:27.640
Because it was just so weird to pull up to that building.

205
00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:28.860
Oh, my gosh.

206
00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:37.120
And there were a lot of, you know, there were a lot of major chapters in our lives that

207
00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:45.300
people went through, marriages, divorces, children, all these kind of major life events

208
00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:46.080
that were happening.

209
00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:50.020
And sometimes I'll see an episode, and I'll be like, oh, yeah, that's when so-and-so was

210
00:10:50.020 --> 00:10:54.900
going through this, or that's when so-and-so child was born.

211
00:10:54.900 --> 00:11:00.040
Or, you know, and that stuff's really beautiful to remember.

212
00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:07.340
Even the parts that might have been painful chapters, they're still merely meaningful chapters

213
00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:09.500
in our lives and histories.

214
00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:14.460
And it's just so wild to have this show that's kind of a bookmark, right?

215
00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:16.160
You can just go to any point.

216
00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:24.320
I mean, for us, having made the show, I can recall things that I was experiencing when I

217
00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:35.000
see a scene or if I catch a chunk of an episode, I can remember where I was in my life a little

218
00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:35.660
bit more.

219
00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:38.820
That is so true.

220
00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:40.100
Yeah, it's really wild.

221
00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:46.600
And, you know, and like I said, even the ups and downs of it, it's all beautiful, right?

222
00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:55.320
And just to have those triggers available to remember those things, it feels very, yeah,

223
00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:58.000
it just feels very, very beautiful overall.

224
00:11:58.920 --> 00:11:59.940
Yeah, yeah.

225
00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:05.460
This, as you were saying, is such a big storyline for you, traveling salesman leading into the

226
00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:11.000
return and were you super excited to get this script to see what all you got to do and play

227
00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:13.720
with Steve and like, like you got to in this episode?

228
00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:15.300
Yeah, of course.

229
00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:18.780
I mean, it was still early enough for me.

230
00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:22.680
I mean, I, every table read is a joy, right?

231
00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:25.380
It's just so much discovery for the, for the caSt.

232
00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:29.460
The writers have been kind of in the trenches for a couple of weeks or months at that point

233
00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:30.620
with every episode.

234
00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:33.840
But for us, it's like this fresh, exciting thing.

235
00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:42.240
And I do, I do remember feeling, you know, as I got more sort of meat on the bone and some

236
00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:50.520
of those later episodes, it just felt, it really felt like an affirmation that Andy was a, was

237
00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:56.900
a valuable part of this, you know, cast overall and of the show.

238
00:12:56.900 --> 00:13:03.340
And, um, so yeah, there were, there was always a kind of giddy anticipation with those table reads.

239
00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:04.840
Like, oh, what am I going to get to do?

240
00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:07.520
What did the writers come up with, you know?

241
00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:11.880
And then of course, what did the, how is Steve going to make me laugh this week?

242
00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:12.640
Right.

243
00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:21.400
And this episode opens with your phone ringing and there's like a four part harmony of you singing.

244
00:13:21.400 --> 00:13:22.200
Yeah.

245
00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:23.420
